# Freud - 1917 - Mourning and Melancholia

- **related** [[Depression]], [[Unipolar Depression and Major Depressive Disorder]]
- **Core concepts (to clarify) on first pass:**
	- Meta-psychology and topography of the mind into the Ego and the Critical Faculty
	- Principles of libidinal energy and "mental economics", i.e. all mental acts is distribution of libidinal energy
	- Phenomenological similarities and critical differences of mourning vs melancholia (difference mainly in ?negative cognition)
	- Fall in self-esteem in melancholia as the key difference - the world being poor and empty in mourning, in melancholia it is the ego that feels impoverished (negative cognition)
	- Loss as a driver of both mourning and melancholia, difference being whether the loss is conscious or unconsciousness
	- Unconscious nature of loss in melancholia results in the puzzling nature of melancholic inhibition (e.g. not fully explainable even if an object of loss is identified, as the individual has yet to come to terms with what is actually lossed)
	- Normal work of mourning
	- Work of melancholia - identification of the ego as if it were the lost object ("the shadow of the object fell upon the ego")
	- Necessary criterias for melancholia, namely loss of the object, ambivalence, and regression of libido into the ego
	- Nature of self-reproach/ negative cognition in the melancholiac vs those who normally devour in remorse and self-reproach
	- Role of ambivalence
	- Role of narcissistic object-choice and regression from narcissistic object-choice to narcissism
	- Open wound and energy depletion
	- Regression to oral cannabalistic phase
	- Connection to early-morning waking, sleep disturbance as effects of somatic vs psychological factors
	- Sadism and tendency of suicide in the melanchoiac (?two fold fate of erotic cathesxis of the object)
	- Manic transition through hypotehsis of economic release
- **Phenomenological similarities of mourning and melancholia** (corresponds to items of diagnostic criterias) - Freud attributes these to inhibition and circumscription in the ego as exclusive devotion to the internal process of mourning and melancholia, leaving insufficient energy for other purposes:
	- Painful dejection
	- Abrogation of interest in the outside world
	- Loss of the capacity of love
	- Inhibition of all activities
- **One phenomenological exception between mourning and melancholia** - likely due to predisposition (?ambivalence, narcissistic object choice and identification), despite similar trigger:
	- Inhibition can be somewhat fully explained in grief, where as severity of inhibition appears puzzling in the melancholiac for our lack of awareness of the unconscious loss that has been labouring the patient
	- <u>Fall in self esteem central in melancholia</u>: "In grief the world becomes poor and empty; in melancholia it is the ego itself."
	- <u>The fall in self esteem, is often moral, and is often communicated</u> - with lack of shame ("the plaint"), where in normal state of remorse, a person hinds its head: "The patient represents his ego to us as worthless, incapable of any efforts and morally dispicable; he reproaches himself, vilifies himself and expects to be cast out and chastised. He abases himself before everone and commiserates his own relatives for being connected with someone so unworthy. He does not realize that any change has taken place in him, but extends his self-criticism back over the past and declares that he was never any better. This delusional belittling - which is predominantly moral - is completed by sleeplessness and refusal of nourishment, and by an overthrow, psychologically very remarkable, of tha tinstinct which constrains every life thing to cling to life." "In the clinical picture of melancholia dissatisfaction with the self on moral grounds is far the most outstanding feature; the self-critcism much less frequently concerns itself with bodily infirmity, ugliness, weakness, social inferiority; among these latter ills that the patient dreads or asseverates the thought of poverty along has a favoured position." "Shame before others, whic would characterize this condition above everything, is lacking in him, or at least there is little sign of it."
	- <u>Self-reproaches is not necessarily wrong</u> - therapeutically and scientifically useless to challenge such self-convictions, it is not the content of self-reproach that is pathological, but the emotional weight and expectation of punishment attached to it: "In certain other self-accusations he also seems to us justified, only that he has a keener eye for the truth than others who are not melancholic." "For all we know it may be that he has come very near to self-knowedge; we only wonder why a man must become ill before he can discover truth of this kind. For there can be no doubt that whoever holds and expresses to others such an opinion of himself - one that Hamlet harboured of himself and all men - that man is ill, wehether he speaks the truth or is more or less unfair to himself"
	- Wondering if this is universally true, e.g. someone who lost lots of money from business misventure might also present with self-reproach along with other features… does this imply melancholia
	- Somatic complements in melancholia may differ that from mourning (to be explored later)
- **Economics of the mind:**
	- Cathexis (Besetzung), means an occupation, the volume of mental energy (libido) attached to a specific memory, person or idea
	- Finite in nature and must be allocated
	- Overconsumption for one process (esp. during hypercathexis during grief processing) renders little energy to be distributed to other tasks
- **Work of mourning** (metapsychology of mourning) - process of "ego-healing after a loss":
  - Trigger being the loss of an object, a loved one, an ideal etc.
  - Frequent reality testing, which reveals the object is undoubtedly gone
  - Withdrawal of libidinal energy from the lost objects, redirected to another object
  - Process is laboured and delayed (e.g. turning away from reality through denial, or even "hallucinatory wish-psychosis") but reality eventually gains the day
  - Also describes why the process takes time, the command for detachment of libidinal energy from the lost object is not immediately obeyed (clinging):
    - Every single memory, hope and tie bound to the person in memory is brought up, and "hyper-cathected" (focused on intensely)
    - Bit-by-bit detatchment at under "great expense of time and cathectic energy".

    "It may be universally observed that man never willingly abandons a libido-position, not even when a substitute is already beckoning to him."
  - Use of cathetic energy underlies the features especially inhibition.
  - Pain in terms of economics of the mind probably important here
  - Process is painful, not at all easily explainable by mental economics, but very important is that "the pain seems natural to the outsider"
  - The ego becomes free and uninhibited eventually
- **Work of melancholia** - phenomenologically appears rather 1) extra-ordinarily painful, and 2) puzzling inhibition, and 3) a/w self-reproach:
  - The loss is not fully conscious, often attributed to an unconscious loss, even if a loss is observed at the superficial level" "In yet other cases one feels justified in concluding that a loss of the kind has been exeperienced, but one cannot see clearly what has been lost, and may the more readily suppose that the patient cannot consciously perceive what it is he has lost."
  - Often it appears as extra-ordinarily painful, inhibition being more severe than what is to be expected from a superficial level, and most important, a loss of self esteem that is in some sense truthful, and shameless. This is when we expect a melancholic process.
  - Key observations - shifted reproach:
    - Self-reproach is key here, because it is contradictory, we expect that the loss is external, but somehow the patient describes some process is internal.
    - To make an assumption, self-reproach reflects a change in constitution of the ego, i.e. splitting of the ego, <u>we must assume that the mind consists of a critical isntitution that separates it from the rest of the ego, which is conscience</u>: "One part of the ego sets itself over against the otherl judges it critically, and, as it were, looks upon it as an object". "Our suspicion that the critical institution in the mind which is split off from the ego (later termed the superego) might also demonstrate its independence in other circumstances will be confirmed by all further observations. We shall really find justification for distinguishing this institution from the rest of the ego.:
    - "It is the mental faculty commonly called conscience that we are thus recognizing, we shall count it, along with the censorship of consciousness and the testing of reality, among the great institutions of the ego and shall also find evidence elsewhere showing that it can become diseased independently."
    - To reconstruct this process, lets assume that these self-reproaches are reproaches to the reproaches against a loved object which has been shifted to the patient's own ego.
  - Simply a hypothesis of the contradiction - how loss of the object transforms into loss of the ego: "The object-cathexis proved to have little power of resistance, and was abandoned; but the free libido was withdrawn into the ego and not directed to another object. It did not find application there, however, in any one of several possible ways, but served simply to establish an identification of the ego with the abandoned object. Thus the shadow of the object fell upon the ego, so that the latter could henceforth be criticized by a special mental faculty like an object, like the forsaken object. In this way the loss of the object became transformed into a loss in the ego, and the conflict between the ego and the loved person transformed into a cleavage between the criticizing faculty of the ego and the ego as altered by the identification".
    - Predisposed by a fragile attachment to the object, such that bit-by-bit detatchment does not occur ("The object-cathexis proved to have little power of resistance, and was abandoned"), this observation is paradoxical as the contents of the melancholia reveals that the patient seems to be obsessed, however they have actually simply abandonned the attachment instead of fighting for the relationship.
    - Somewhat obsessional nature of the melancholiac reflected by the internalisation of the loss object into the entire ego, the object is gone from reality, but its "shadow" (mental representation) is now cast over the patient's entire ego, i.e. the ego identifies with the lost person and becomes the subject of "plaint"
  - Necessary criterias for work of melancholia - 1) loss of object, 2) ambivalence, and 3) narcissism (narcissm provides the mechanism of regression, ambivalence provides the ammunition)
- **Contradiction between obsessional nature towards the object but the object is easily abandonned in melancholia:** "On one hand, a strong fixation to the love-object must have been present; on the other hand, in contradiction to this, the object cathexis have had ltitle power of resistance."
  - This implies (as Otto Rank aptly remarked), that "the oject-choice had been effected on a narcissistic basis, so that when obstacle arise in the way of the object-cathexis it can regress into narcissism".
  - The love-object becomes replaced by the narcissistic identification of the object as the object of erotic cathexis
- **Narcissistic object-choice and narcisism:**
  - Narcissistic object choice as a pre-requisite for melancholia, choosing an object (person or ideal), because they represent who you are, who you were, or who you want to be (the object is in essense a mirror)
  - Loss of the object will result in narcissistic identification of the ego w/ the abandonned object (i.e. "cannabalistic incorporation"), defensive maneuver to ensure that love-relation need not be given up, as the object is now well alive within ego
  - All love, as well as all hate previously expressed towards the love object will be expressed towards one self
- **Ambivalence and two fold fate** - reduction to sadism:
  - Narcissism + Loss ≠ Melancholia
  - If a relationship were truly 100% "good" (pure love without a trace of frustration or hate), the loss would likely result in profound Mourning. The libido would eventually detach bit-by-bit because there is no reason for the "critical faculty" to attack the memory.
  - Shadow of the object which contains ambivalent relations, has beenc casted over the ego, where the love continues to drive the narcissistic identification of the object (despite being abandoned already), while the hate becomes a front of attack by the conscience. "If the object-love, which cannot be given up, takes refuge in narcissistic identification, while the object itself is abandonned, then hate is expended upon this new substitute-object \[the ego\], railing at it, depreciating it, making it suffer and deriving sadistic gratification from its suffering".
  - This also explains why self-reproach invariably lacks shame, because they are directed towards the internalised object (sadistic gratification): "The self-torments of melancholiacs, which are without doubt pleasurable, signify, just like the corresponding phenomenon in the obsessional neurosis, a gratification of sadistic tendancies and of hate, both of which relate to an object and in this way have both been turned round upon the self."
- **Mechanisms of suicide:**
  - Can be thought as two opposing forces: one of self-love, and one as hate
  - Freud admits that the ego's self love is usually so vast that it is difficult to conceive how it could connive at its own destruction, arguing that suicide only becomes possible through the two fold fate:
    - Being treated as an object, when it is able to "launch against itself the animosity relating to an object - that primordial reaction on the part of the ego to all objects of the outer world."
    - Vengeful, murderous impulses being redirected towards the ego, i.e. the ego is overwhelmed by the object in two ultimately different ways.

    "Thus regression from narcissistic object-choice the object is indeed abolished, but in spite of all proves itself stronger than the ego's self. In the two contrasting situations of intense love and of suicide the ego is overwhelmed by the object, though in totally different ways."
- **Other unresolved aspects of melancholia:**
  - Manifestations of dread of poverty in ?anal erotism torn out of its context and altered by regression
  - Untreated melancholia can resolve on its own as time elapse without leaving traces of any gross changes:
    - But unlike the process of mourning where the bit by bit painful detatchment occurs from the command after testing of reality
    - We can infer that the ego is probably undergoing some analgous task during the course of melancholia
  - Open wound analogy:
    - The complex of melancholia works as an open wound, and much libidinal energy is necessary to limit the damage done (anticathexis as energy spent for defense mechanisms)
    - Underlies psychomotor retardation
    - Underlies insomnia: loss of general awareness of the need to sleep, and hence insomnia, early morning awakening. Improved symptomatology during the evening (i.e. loss of diurnal variation) is more attributed to somatic factors (?sleepiness)
## 1. Overview
- Mourning: a conscious, reality‑based process of withdrawing libido from a lost object and eventually redirecting it outward; typically reparative and time‑limited.
- Melancholia: a pathological response in which loss (often unconscious) leads to introjection/identification with the lost object, collapse of self‑esteem, and punitive self‑reproach.

## 2. Core clinical similarities
- Both show painful dejection, loss of interest in the outside world, inability to love, and inhibition of activity.
- Shared mechanism: heavy libidinal investment (cathexis) on the lost object reduces available psychic energy for other functions.

## 3. Key differences
- Consciousness of the loss
	- Mourning: loss is known and reality‑tested.
	- Melancholia: loss may be unconscious or unnamed.
- Locus of suffering
	- Mourning: “the world becomes poor and empty.”
	- Melancholia: “it is the ego itself that is impoverished.”
- Self‑attitude
	- Mourning: normal grief, occasional regret.
	- Melancholia: pervasive self‑reproach and devaluation of the self.
- Libidinal economy / mechanism
	- Mourning: libido is gradually withdrawn from the object and can be reattached elsewhere.
	- Melancholia: libido is withdrawn into the ego; identification with the lost object occurs.
- Role of ambivalence and narcissistic object‑choice
	- Mourning: may or may not involve ambivalence.
	- Melancholia: requires ambivalence (love + hate) and often follows narcissistic object‑choice; hostile impulses are introjected and turned against the ego.
- Clinical severity
	- Mourning: painful but typically resolves with time and support.
	- Melancholia: deeper inhibition, psychomotor changes, sleep disturbance, somatic complaints, and elevated suicide risk.

## 4. Mechanisms and theory (concepts)
- Cathexis and economy
	- Excessive cathexis of the object leaves diminished libido for other psychic tasks, producing withdrawal and inhibition.
- Introjection / identification
	- In melancholia the lost object’s attributes are taken into the ego; the ego bears the object’s “shadow.”
- Ambivalence
	- Necessary for the self‑reproach of melancholia: hatred toward the object, once introjected, becomes hostility toward the ego.
	- Ambivalence can be unconscious — absence of conscious hatred does not exclude introjected hostility.
- Narcissistic object‑choice
	- Definition: choosing objects because they function as mirrors/extensions of the self (provide admiration, bolster self‑esteem) rather than primarily offering care or support.
	- Implication: losing such an object threatens core self‑regard; combined with ambivalence, loss can produce self‑hatred.
- Superego / internalized critical object
	- A punitive internalized object can mediate severe self‑reproach even if the patient cannot explicitly identify the loss or hatred.

## 5. Clarifying paragraph on narcissistic object‑choice
- A narcissistic object‑choice means the object served as a mirror for self‑esteem or as an ego‑ideal. Because the object functioned to reflect and bolster self, its loss is experienced as a loss of self. Where ambivalence exists (even unconsciously), the hostile impulse toward the object may be introjected; identification with the object causes the ego to be felt as guilty, worthless, or reproachable. If genuine hostility/ambivalence is absent at all levels, loss of a narcissistic object more commonly produces mourning rather than melancholia.

## 6. Clinical implications and management
- Mourning
	- Provide containment and reality‑testing; time and supportive relationships usually enable detachment and reinvestment.
- Melancholia
	- Therapeutic tasks: differentiate ego from object, bring unconscious ambivalence into awareness, reduce pathological self‑reproach, strengthen ego boundaries.
	- Monitor suicide risk; address somatic and psychomotor symptoms as part of care.
- Assessment tips
	- Look for fragile self‑esteem, need for mirroring, hidden ambivalence, and a punitive internalized object when distinguishing melancholia from mourning.

## 8. Key Freud passages
- “In mourning the world becomes poor and empty; in melancholia it is the ego itself.”
- “The self‑reproach, the devaluation of the self, is the most conspicuous manifestation of melancholia.”

## 9. Clinical vignette
- A patient who built identity around a partner’s admiration (narcissistic object) loses that partner.
- Although the patient reports only love, therapy uncovers hidden anger.
- Identification with the lost partner and introjection of hostility produce overwhelming self‑loathing and withdrawal — a melancholic reaction rather than ordinary mourning.